meta {
  name: Get Summary
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/owner-dashboard/summary?range=lastMonth
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  range: lastMonth
  ~from: 2026-08-01
  ~to: 2026-08-13
  ~producerIds: 
  ~leadSourceIds: {{mailerLeadSourceId}}
  ~policyTypes: Auto,Home
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerDashboardToken}}
}

script:post-response {
  // The other two reads must agree with this one — they assert against it.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const body = res.getBody();
    bru.setVar("ownerSummaryPremium", body.premium.current);
    bru.setVar("ownerSummaryItems", body.items.current);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("echoes the window it used and the one it compared with", function () {
    const { period } = res.getBody();
    expect(period.key).to.equal("lastMonth");
    expect(period.current.from).to.match(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-01$/);
    expect(period.previous.from).to.match(/^\d{4}-\d{2}-01$/);
    // Last month against the month before it — never overlapping.
    expect(period.previous.to < period.current.from).to.equal(true);
  });

  test("every KPI is a trend: current, previous, change, unit, status", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    for (const key of ["premium", "items", "avgPremiumPerHousehold", "closingRatio"]) {
      expect(body[key]).to.include.all.keys("current", "previous", "change", "unit", "status");
      expect(["ok", "no_prior_data"]).to.include(body[key].status);
    }
  });

  test("a ratio moves in points, an amount in percent", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.premium.unit).to.equal("percent");
    expect(body.closingRatio.unit).to.equal("points");
  });

  test("no prior data means no change — never a number", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    for (const key of ["premium", "items", "avgPremiumPerHousehold", "closingRatio"]) {
      if (body[key].status === "no_prior_data") {
        expect(body[key].change).to.equal(null);
        expect(body[key].previous).to.equal(null);
      }
    }
  });

  test("the closing ratio carries both of its sides, or says why it has none", function () {
    const ratio = res.getBody().closingRatio;
    expect(ratio.soldPremium).to.be.a("number");
    expect(ratio.quotedPremium).to.be.a("number");
    if (ratio.current === null) {
      expect(["no_quotes", "too_few_quotes"]).to.include(ratio.reason);
    } else {
      expect(ratio.reason).to.equal(null);
    }
  });

  test("the LOB mix names at most three types and closes at 100", function () {
    const mix = res.getBody().lobMix;
    expect(mix.top.length).to.be.at.most(3);
    if (mix.policyCount > 0) {
      const shown = mix.top.reduce((sum, slice) => sum + slice.pct, 0);
      expect(Math.round((shown + mix.otherPct) * 10) / 10).to.equal(100);
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /owner-dashboard/summary — the Owner View KPI row (PAC-135)

  Total Bound Premium · Items Bound / LOB Mix · Avg Premium per Household ·
  Agency Closing Ratio — each for the selected window **and** for the window it
  is compared with.

  - **Module:** `owner_dashboard` (must be enabled for the agency)
  - **Permission:** `owner_dashboard:read`
  - **Data scope:** clamped by `buildScopeFilter`, like `GET /performance`. An
    agency-scoped caller sees the agency; a branch-scoped one their branch
    (`X-Branch-Id`); `producerIds` can narrow either and widen neither.

  > **Why not `leaderboard:read`?** `GET /leaderboard` is *aggregates only* — a
  > producer never sees a colleague's dollars, and the e2e suite asserts it. The
  > owner's view is per-producer dollars, so it lives behind its own permission
  > rather than widening a contract producers rely on.

  ## Query — shared by all three `/owner-dashboard/*` reads
  One schema on purpose: the three reads promise to agree, which is only true if
  they can never be asked slightly different questions.

  | param | type | default | notes |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | range | enum | `mtd` | `mtd` · `lastMonth` · `last3Months` · `ytd` · `lastYear` · `custom` |
  | from, to | `YYYY-MM-DD` | — | required with `custom`; Chicago calendar days, `to` inclusive, ≤ 366 days |
  | producerIds | id[] | — | user ids; narrow-only |
  | leadSourceIds | id[] | — | from `GET /lead-sources`, and/or `__none__` for "no source" |
  | policyTypes | enum[] | — | line of business = the Sold form's Policy Type options |

  Every multi-select takes the repeated (`a=1&a=2`), comma-separated (`a=1,2`)
  or single form, and an empty one means **no filter**, never "match nothing".

  ## The windows
  | range | window | compared with |
  |---|---|---|
  | `mtd` | 1st → today | the **same elapsed days** of last month (Sep 1–21 → Aug 1–21) |
  | `lastMonth` | last calendar month | the month before it |
  | `last3Months` | the 3 complete months before this one | the 3 before those |
  | `ytd` | Jan 1 → today | Jan 1 → the same day, last year |
  | `lastYear` | last calendar year | the year before it |
  | `custom` | as given | the preceding span of equal length |

  Presets shift by their calendar unit; only `custom` uses "the preceding N
  days". For `mtd` that rule would compare Sep 1–21 with Aug 11–31, and against
  the whole of August the badge would read red until the month ended. A day that
  does not exist in the earlier month clamps (Mar 31 → all of February).

  ## Sold figures come from policy lines
  A deal is one sale and may hold several policies: Auto $1,200 + Home $1,800 is
  one $3,000 deal. Line of business is the *policy's* type, so `policyTypes=Auto`
  counts **$1,200**, not $3,000. Every sold figure is summed from the deal's
  `policies` rows.

  A record with no lines — 10 migrated deals, and every migrated quote recap —
  counts in full with no LOB filter; under one it is kept when its own type list
  *contains* a selected type, for its whole amount. LOB-filtered **quoted**
  premium is therefore approximate on historic data.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "period": {
      "key": "lastMonth",
      "current":  { "from": "2026-08-01", "to": "2026-08-31" },
      "previous": { "from": "2026-07-01", "to": "2026-07-31" }
    },
    "premium": { "current": 185834.12, "previous": 286633.4, "change": -35.2, "unit": "percent", "status": "ok" },
    "items":   { "current": 130, "previous": 171, "change": -24, "unit": "percent", "status": "ok" },
    "avgPremiumPerHousehold": { "current": 3643.81, "previous": 3722.51, "change": -2.1, "unit": "percent", "status": "ok" },
    "closingRatio": {
      "current": 112.9, "previous": 88.2, "change": 24.7, "unit": "points", "status": "ok",
      "soldPremium": 185834.12, "quotedPremium": 164594, "reason": null
    },
    "lobMix": {
      "policyCount": 92,
      "top": [
        { "policyType": "Home", "policyCount": 46, "pct": 50 },
        { "policyType": "Auto", "policyCount": 41, "pct": 44.6 },
        { "policyType": "Landlord", "policyCount": 2, "pct": 2.2 }
      ],
      "otherPct": 3.2
    }
  }
  ```

  | field | notes |
  |---|---|
  | `*.status` | `no_prior_data` = the comparison window held no sales at all (the business may not have existed). `change` and `previous` are then `null` — a UI state, never `+∞%` |
  | `*.change` | `percent`: relative change. `points`: difference, for a figure that is already a percentage — 50% → 55% is **+5 points**, not +10% |
  | `items` | items, not policies — three cars on one auto policy is three |
  | `avgPremiumPerHousehold` | sold premium ÷ distinct households with a sale; `null` at zero. Same ladder as the producer scorecard, so the two screens cannot disagree |
  | `closingRatio.current` | sold ÷ quoted premium × 100. **May exceed 100**: a period's sales are not only that period's quotes |
  | `closingRatio.reason` | `no_quotes`, or `too_few_quotes` — fewer than one quote recap per ten sales. Recaps were barely recorded before 2026 (9 against 1,112 deals in 2025), and dividing one by the other is a five-figure percentage, not a closing rate |
  | `lobMix.top[].pct` | share of **policies** sold — not items, not premium. An uncatalogued legacy code counts as a policy but is never a named slice |

  ## Errors
  | status | when |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | unknown `range` (including the producer dashboard's `today` / `week`); `custom` without both bounds, reversed, impossible, or over 366 days; a malformed id; a `policyTypes` value that is not on the Sold form |
  | 401 | no / expired token |
  | 403 | no `owner_dashboard:read`, or the module is off for the agency |
}
